---

In a #small saucepan{}, melt @butter{3.5%oz}(unsalted, cubed, about 7 tbsp) over medium-low heat.

When the butter is completely melted, add @flour{3.5%oz}. With a blunt-end wooden spatula, stir to combine the butter and flour.

Soon, the butter and flour will fuse and swell. For the next ~{20-25%minutes}, reduce to low heat and stir constantly because the roux burns easily. 

> If the roux starts separating, switch to a whisk and mix vigorously.

The roux will turn golden brown quickly toward the end.

Add @curry powder{4%Tbsp}(S&B preferred), @garam masala{1%Tbsp}, and @?cayenne pepper{1/2%tsp} to the roux. 

Cook and stir for ~{30%seconds} and remove from the heat. 

> You can use the roux immediately at this point, but usually, we continue with molding it into a block.

Transfer the roux to a glass or metal container lined with parchment paper. Let it cool completely on the kitchen counter, then refrigerate to solidify overnight.

With a sharp knife, cut the block into 8 cubes, about 1 x 1 inch (2.5 x 2.5 cm) each. Transfer the curry roux squares to a glass container. This will keep for 1 month in the fridge of 3-4 months in the freezer.

> As this roux is unsalted, remember to add salt. About 2-4 tsp per serving (one block). Or just use salted butter and avoid this.

> Roughly 6-7 cubes of this recipe are equivalent to one box of store-bought roux (the ones that require 4 cups of water/broth).